  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSynoptically, on Sunday the upper level ridge begins to set up over \nthe SE CONUS while a trough begins to move into the Pacific \nNorthwest. This change will transition our upper level flow to be \nprimarily out of the west to southwest. During this period, \nshortwaves are expected to move through the area, potentially \nincreasing precipation chances. \n\nThroughout the period, high temperatures are forecast to be mainly \nin the 90s. Sunday is currently forecast to be the warmest day,\nwith highs in the 90s for the majority of the CWA. Areas east \nof Hwy 83 have the best chance of seeing triple digits \ntemperatures on Sunday. The \"coolest\" day is currently forecast \nto be Monday, with highs in the mid 80s to low 90s. Winds are \nexpected to mainly remain calm through the early part of the \nweek. \n\nRegarding precipitation, there is potential for showers and storms \neach afternoon. Sunday and Monday currently have the highest \npotential with PoPs, ranging from 25-50% across the area. As for \nsevere potential, while shear values appear to be decent, guidance \nsuggests that CAPE values may limit storm development. This could \nresult in more showers than storms. From Tuesday onward, \nprecipitation chances begin to taper off, with PoPs in the \n10-20% range.",
